 ],
 "title": "Sulfur Metabolism in Yeast: A Complex Network of Genes and Chemicals",
 "summary": "This cluster revolves around the sulfur metabolism in yeast, specifically in the organisms S. cerevisiae LMD17 and RM11. The genes MET17 and HSU1 play crucial roles in this process, with MET17 catalyzing the conversion of homocysteine to sulfate and HSU1 having various activities, including the release of sulfide. The deletion of these genes, met17Δ and hsu1Δ, leads to inefficient sulfide assimilation and sulfur starvation, respectively. The chemicals involved in this process include sulfate, sulfide, hydrogen sulfide, and methanethiol, which are all interconnected through various reactions and conversions.",
